      date: "2018",
      phase: "revival",
      headline: "Back to the plant — and to the microbe",
      narrative: "The newest indigo borrows the plant's own trick. In 2018 a Berkeley team made E. coli produce indican, the leaf's protected precursor, so that dyeing needs neither aniline nor a reducing agent. Farms grow plant indigo again, from Tennessee's Stony Creek Colors to Bangladesh's Living Blue, and UNESCO listed Europe's indigo resist printing in 2018. Plant indigo is still estimated at about 1% of supply.",
